Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from the book by Charles Dickens
Draft date: 12/15/2024
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action Victorian period drama; naturalistic faces, fabric, fog, and candlelight. No stylized illustration.

FADE IN:

EXT. LONDON STREETS AND COURT - CHRISTMAS EVE AFTERNOON

Dense brown fog chokes the narrow court. People wheeze, stamp feet, beat hands on breasts. Candles flare in office windows like ruddy smears on the palpable air.

(SOUND: muffled coughs, distant church bell, feet stamping on stone)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead, to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING-HOUSE - CONTINUOUS

The firm name SCROOGE AND MARLEY still stands above the warehouse door. Inside, EBENEZER SCROOGE—elderly, thin, pointed nose, shrivelled cheek, frosty rime on head and eyebrows, red-rimmed eyes, thin blue lips—sits at his desk in dark severe business coat and waistcoat. A very small fire burns. The door stands open to the Tank.

(SOUND: scratch of quill, faint crackle of meagre coal)

In the Tank, BOB CRATCHIT—thin, threadbare grey-brown coat, long white comforter dangling—copies letters by a fire of one coal, trying to warm his hands at a candle.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was his sole executor, sole administrator, sole friend, and sole mourner. Oh! But he was a tight-fisted hand at the grind-stone.

EXT. COURT OUTSIDE COUNTING-HOUSE - CONTINUOUS

Blind men's dogs tug owners into doorways at Scrooge's approach in memory; no one stops him with gladsome looks.

(SOUND: dog whimper, fog-muffled traffic)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Even the blind men's dogs appeared to know him. Nobody ever stopped him in the street to say, "My dear Scrooge, how are you?"

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING-HOUSE - CONTINUOUS

The outer door bursts open. FRED—young, handsome, ruddy face all in a glow, eyes sparkling, breath smoking—enters in winter coat and bright muffler.

(SOUND: door bang, rush of cold air)

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

Scrooge waves him off, grating voice cold.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
I'll see you in that extremity first. Why did you get married?

FRED
Because I fell in love.

SCROOGE
Because you fell in love! Good afternoon!

FRED
I want nothing from you. Why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
A Merry Christmas, uncle! And A Happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves without an angry word. At the outer door he greets Bob warmly. Bob returns it cordially, colder than Scrooge yet warmer in heart.

(SOUND: door close, Bob's faint cheerful reply)

SCROOGE
(muttering)
My clerk, with fifteen shillings a week, and a wife and family, talking about a merry Christmas. I'll retire to Bedlam.

Two PORTLY GENTLEMEN—pleasant, hats off, books and papers—enter and bow.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
We have no doubt his liberality is well represented by his surviving partner. At this festive season it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 2
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
Both very busy, sir.

SCROOGE
Oh! I was afraid something had occurred to stop them in their useful course. I'm very glad to hear it.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
A few of us are endeavouring to raise a fund to buy the Poor some meat and drink, and means of warmth. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. I don't make merry myself at Christmas, and I can't afford to make idle people merry. Those who are badly off must go there.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 2
Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. It's not my business. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The gentlemen withdraw. Scrooge resumes his labours with an improved opinion of himself.

(SOUND: quill scratch resumes, fog horn distant)

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING-HOUSE - LATER

The hour of shutting up arrives. Scrooge dismounts his stool with ill will. Bob snuffs his candle and puts on his hat.

(SOUND: stool scrape, candle snuff)

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. If I was to stop half-a-crown for it, you'd think yourself ill-used.

Bob smiles faintly.

SCROOGE
And yet you don't think me ill-used when I pay a day's wages for no work. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob promises. Scrooge buttons his great-coat to the chin and walks out with a growl.

EXT. CORNHILL / LONDON STREETS - EVENING

Bob, white comforter dangling below his waist, no great-coat, goes down a slide on Cornhill twenty times with a lane of boys, then runs home to Camden Town as hard as he can pelt.

(SOUND: ice scrape, boys' shouts, running feet)

Scrooge takes his melancholy dinner in a tavern, reads newspapers, beguiles the evening with his banker's-book, then walks home through fog and frost.

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS YARD - NIGHT

A gloomy suite in a lowering pile up a dark yard. Fog and frost hang about the black old gateway. Scrooge gropes with his hands, key in the lock of the door.

(SOUND: wind moan, distant drip, key rattle)

The large knocker—nothing particular about it—becomes MARLEY'S FACE: dismal light like a bad lobster in a dark cellar, ghostly spectacles turned up on ghostly forehead, hair stirred, eyes wide and motionless, livid colour.

Scrooge stares. It is a knocker again.

(SOUND: sudden silence, then his sharp breath)

INT. SCROOGE'S GLOOMY CHAMBERS - CONTINUOUS

Scrooge turns the key sturdily, walks in, lights his candle. He looks cautiously behind the door—only screws and nuts. He says "Pooh, pooh!" and closes it with a bang. The sound resounds like thunder.

(SOUND: door bang, echoing peals through house)

He double-locks himself in—not his custom. Checks sitting-room, bedroom, lumber-room: nobody under table or sofa, small fire, spoon and basin, little saucepan of gruel on the hob, nobody under bed or in closet. He takes off cravat, puts on faded dark dressing-gown, slippers, and nightcap, sits before the low fire to take his gruel.

(SOUND: fire spit, gruel simmer faint)

The Dutch tiles of the old fireplace illustrate Scriptures, yet Marley's face comes like the ancient Prophet's rod and swallows them all.

SCROOGE
Humbug!

He walks across the room, sits again. His glance rests on a disused bell. It begins to swing softly, then rings out loudly—and so does every bell in the house.

(SOUND: soft swing rising to wild peal of every bell)

The bells cease. A clanking noise deep below, as if someone drags a heavy chain over casks in the wine-merchant's cellar. The cellar-door flies open with a booming sound. The noise comes up the stairs, straight toward his door.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

Without a pause it comes on through the heavy door. The dying flame leaps up as though it cried "I know him! Marley's Ghost!" and falls again.

JACOB MARLEY—same face, pigtail, usual waistcoat, tights and boots, tassels bristling, transparent so the two buttons on his coat behind show, chain of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, heavy purses clasped about his middle—stands before him. Death-cold eyes. Folded kerchief bound about head and chin. Hair and skirts agitated as by hot vapour.

(SOUND: chain clank and drag, low moan of air)

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then? You're particular, for a shade.

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
Can you—can you sit down?

MARLEY
I can.

Marley sits on the opposite side of the fireplace.

MARLEY
You don't believe in me.

SCROOGE
I don't.

MARLEY
Why do you doubt your senses?

SCROOGE
Because a little thing affects them. A slight disorder of the stomach makes them cheats. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

Marley raises a frightful cry and shakes his chain. Scrooge holds on tight to his chair. Marley takes off the bandage; his lower jaw drops upon his breast.

(SOUND: appalling chain rattle, jaw clack)

Scrooge falls upon his knees.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY
Man of the worldly mind! Do you believe in me or not?

SCROOGE
I do. I must. But why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

MARLEY
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen. If that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

Marley wrings his shadowy hands and shakes the chain again.

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard. Is its pattern strange to you?

Scrooge trembles more and more.

MARLEY
Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself? It was full as heavy and as long as this, seven Christmas Eves ago. You have laboured on it since. It is a ponderous chain!

SCROOGE
Jacob. Old Jacob Marley, tell me more. Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY
I have none to give. I cannot rest, I cannot stay, I cannot linger anywhere. My spirit never walked beyond our counting-house—mark me!—in life. Wearisome journeys lie before me!

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY
Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business.

He holds up the chain and flings it heavily upon the ground.

(SOUND: heavy chain crash)

MARLEY
At this time of the rolling year, I suffer most. Why did I walk through crowds of fellow-beings with my eyes turned down, and never raise them to that blessed Star which led the Wise Men to a poor abode!

SCROOGE
Hear me! Don't be hard upon me! Don't be flowery, Jacob! Pray!

MARLEY
I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. A chance and hope of my procuring, Ebenezer.

SCROOGE
You were always a good friend to me. Thank'ee!

MARLEY
You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

Scrooge's countenance falls.

SCROOGE
Is that the chance and hope you mentioned, Jacob? I—I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One.

SCROOGE
Couldn't I take 'em all at once, and have it over, Jacob?

MARLEY
Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more; and look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us!

Marley binds the wrapper round his head again. He walks backward; the window raises itself. He beckons. Confused noises of lamentation fill the air. Marley joins the mournful dirge and floats out upon the bleak dark night.

(SOUND: window rise, wailing spirits, wind)

Scrooge follows to the window. The air is filled with phantoms, wandering in restless haste, every one wearing chains like Marley's. They fade into mist. Scrooge closes the window, examines the double-locked door, tries to say "Humbug!" but stops at the first syllable. He goes straight to bed and falls asleep upon the instant.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT (LATER)

Scrooge awakes in the dark. The neighbouring church chimes the four quarters, then strikes twelve. He touches his repeater: twelve. He scrambles to the window, rubs frost off with his sleeve. Still foggy, extremely cold, no daytime stir.

(SOUND: heavy church bell to twelve, repeater tick)

He lies awake until the quarter chimes. Ding, dong. A quarter past. Half-past. A quarter to. The hour itself—deep, dull, hollow, melancholy ONE. Light flashes up. The curtains of his bed are drawn aside by a hand.

(SOUND: deep ONE, sudden rush of light)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST stands there: strange figure like a child yet like an old man diminished, white hair hanging, unwrinkled face with tenderest bloom, long muscular arms, bare legs and feet, tunic of purest white trimmed with summer flowers, lustrous belt, branch of fresh green holly, bright clear jet of light from crown of head, great extinguisher-cap under arm. The figure fluctuates—now one arm, now twenty legs—then distinct again.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
(soft, low, distant)
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

SCROOGE
I am. Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past. Rise! and walk with me!

Scrooge clasps his robe in supplication as the Spirit makes toward the window.

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, and you shall be upheld in more than this!

The Spirit lays its hand upon his heart. They pass through the wall.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD AND MARKET-TOWN - DAY (PAST)

Clear cold winter day, snow upon the ground. Open country road, fields either hand. The city has vanished. A little market-town appears in the distance with bridge, church, winding river. Shaggy ponies with boys, country gigs and carts. Merry shouts.

(SOUND: crisp air, boys shouting Merry Christmas, pony hooves)

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your lip is trembling. And what is that upon your cheek?

SCROOGE
(catching)
A pimple. Lead me where you will.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been. They have no consciousness of us.

They leave the high-road by a well-remembered lane and approach a mansion of dull red brick, weathercock cupola, broken fortunes, damp mossy walls, broken windows, decayed gates. Fowls in the stables.

INT. DREARY BOARDING SCHOOL - DAY (PAST)

Long bare melancholy room, lines of plain deal forms and desks. A lonely boy—SCROOGE as a child, threadbare school jacket, pale thin face—reads near a feeble fire. Adult Scrooge sits upon a form and weeps.

(SOUND: mouse scuffle behind panelling, drip from water-spout, idle swing of empty door)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

SCROOGE
I know it.

He sobs. Suddenly a man in foreign garments with axe and ass laden with wood appears outside the window—Ali Baba of the boy's reading. Scrooge cries in ecstasy at Valentine and Orson, the Sultan's Groom, the Parrot, Friday.

SCROOGE
Poor boy! I wish... but it's too late now.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
What is the matter?

SCROOGE
Nothing. There was a boy singing a Christmas Carol at my door last night. I should like to have given him something: that's all.

The Ghost smiles and waves its hand.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Let us see another Christmas!

The room grows darker, more dirty. Panels shrink, windows crack. The boy is older, walking up and down despairingly. The door opens. LITTLE FAN—much younger, delicate, child's winter cloak—darts in, puts her arms about his neck, kisses him.

(SOUND: door burst, child's eager laugh)

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, home, home!

BOY SCROOGE
Home, little Fan?

FAN
Yes! Home, for good and all. Home, for ever and ever. Father is so much kinder than he used to be. And you're to be a man! And first we're to be together all the Christmas long, and have the merriest time in all the world.

BOY SCROOGE
You are quite a woman, little Fan!

She drags him toward the door. A terrible voice in the hall cries for Master Scrooge's box. The schoolmaster appears. They drive gaily down the garden-sweep.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature, whom a breath might have withered. But she had a large heart!

SCROOGE
So she had. You're right. I will not gainsay it, Spirit. God forbid!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
True. Your nephew!

Scrooge looks uneasy.

EXT. BUSY CITY THOROUGHFARES - EVENING (PAST)

Shadowy passengers, carts, coaches. Christmas time again; streets lighted up. The Ghost stops at a certain warehouse door.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Do you know it?

SCROOGE
Know it! Was I apprenticed here!

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT (PAST)

Old FEZZIWIG—Welsh wig, capacious waistcoat, jovial—sits behind a high desk. He lays down his pen, looks at the clock pointing to seven, rubs his hands, laughs all over himself.

(SOUND: clock chime, Fezziwig's oily rich laugh)

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick!

Young SCROOGE (apprentice, plain brown coat, darker hair) and DICK WILKINS come briskly in.

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, my boys! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up before a man can say Jack Robinson!

They charge into the street with shutters—up, barred, pinned—back panting. Fezziwig skips down with wonderful agility.

FEZZIWIG
Hilli-ho! Clear away, my lads, and let's have lots of room here! Hilli-ho, Dick! Chirrup, Ebenezer!

Every movable is packed off. Floor swept and watered, lamps trimmed, fuel heaped. The warehouse becomes a snug warm bright ball-room. In comes a fiddler; Mrs. Fezziwig, one vast substantial smile; the three Miss Fezziwigs; six young followers; all the young men and women employed; housemaid, baker, cook, milkman, boy from over the way. Away they all go, twenty couple at once.

(SOUND: fiddle striking up, laughter, feet on boards, pot of porter)

Old Fezziwig dances with Mrs. Fezziwig—advance and retire, both hands, bow and curtsey, corkscrew, thread-the-needle. His calves shine like moons. He cuts so deftly he appears to wink with his legs.

When the clock strikes eleven the ball breaks up. Mr. and Mrs. Fezziwig shake hands with every person and wish a Merry Christmas. The lads are left to their beds under a counter in the back-shop.

During all this Scrooge has acted like a man out of his wits—heart and soul in the scene. He remembers the Ghost looking full upon him, light burning clear.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter to make these silly folks so full of gratitude.

SCROOGE
Small!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Why! Is it not? He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money: three or four perhaps. Is that so much that he deserves this praise?

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome; a pleasure or a toil. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

He feels the Spirit's glance and stops.

SCROOGE
I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

INT. ANOTHER ROOM / OPEN AIR - NIGHT (PAST)

Scrooge sees himself older, in the prime of life, face beginning to wear signs of care and avarice, eager greedy restless eye. He sits by a fair young girl in mourning-dress—BELLE—tears in her eyes sparkling in the Ghost's light.

(SOUND: soft winter quiet, her quiet tears)

BELLE
It matters little. To you, very little. Another idol has displaced me; and if it can cheer and comfort you in time to come as I would have tried to do, I have no just cause to grieve.

YOUNG SCROOGE
What Idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one.

YOUNG SCROOGE
This is the even-handed dealing of the world! There is nothing on which it is so hard as poverty; and there is nothing it professes to condemn with such severity as the pursuit of wealth!

BELLE
You fear the world too much. All your other hopes have merged into the hope of being beyond the chance of its sordid reproach. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. Have I not?

YOUNG SCROOGE
What then? Even if I have grown so much wiser, what then? I am not changed towards you.

BELLE
Our contract is an old one. It was made when we were both poor and content to be so. You are changed. When it was made, you were another man.

YOUNG SCROOGE
I was a boy.

BELLE
Your own feeling tells you that you were not what you are. I am. That which promised happiness when we were one in heart is fraught with misery now that we are two. How often and how keenly I have thought of this, I will not say. It is enough that I have thought of it, and can release you.

YOUNG SCROOGE
Have I ever sought release?

BELLE
In words. No. Never.

YOUNG SCROOGE
In what, then?

BELLE
In a changed nature; in an altered spirit; in another atmosphere of life; another Hope as its great end. If this had never been between us, would you seek me out and try to win me now? Ah, no!

He seems to yield. She turns her head.

BELLE
You may—the memory of what is past half makes me hope you will—have pain in this. A very, very brief time, and you will dismiss the recollection of it gladly, as an unprofitable dream. May you be happy in the life you have chosen!

She leaves him. They part.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more!

SCROOGE
No more! No more. I don't wish to see it. Show me no more!

The relentless Ghost pinions him and forces him to observe.

INT. BELLE'S MODEST COMFORTABLE HOME - NIGHT (PAST)

A room not very large or handsome, but full of comfort. Near the winter fire sits a beautiful young girl so like Belle that Scrooge believes it the same—until he sees her now a comely matron opposite her daughter. Tumultuous children—more than he can count—conduct themselves like forty. Mother and daughter laugh heartily. A knock. The father enters attended by a man laden with Christmas toys and presents. Shouting, struggling, onslaught on the porter. Joy, gratitude, ecstasy.

(SOUND: children's uproar, knock, shouts of wonder)

Later the master of the house sits with daughter and wife at his fireside. Scrooge's sight grows very dim.

HUSBAND
Belle, I saw an old friend of yours this afternoon.

BELLE (MATRON)
Who was it?

HUSBAND
Mr. Scrooge. I passed his office window. His partner lies upon the point of death, I hear; and there he sat alone. Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Remove me from this place. I cannot bear it!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I told you these were shadows of the things that have been. That they are what they are, do not blame me!

SCROOGE
Remove me! I cannot bear it!

He turns upon the Ghost, whose face holds fragments of all the faces it has shown him, and wrestles with it. He seizes the extinguisher-cap and presses it down upon its head. The Spirit drops beneath it; light still streams from under in an unbroken flood upon the ground.

(SOUND: struggle, light hiss)

Scrooge is exhausted, overcome by irresistible drowsiness, in his own bedroom. He reels to bed and sinks into a heavy sleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT (NEXT)

Scrooge awakes in the middle of a prodigiously tough snore. The bell is upon the stroke of One. He pulls all curtains aside himself, establishes a sharp look-out. Five minutes, ten, a quarter of an hour. Nothing. He lies in a blaze of ruddy light from the adjoining room.

(SOUND: tough snore cut short, clock toward One, silence)

He gets up softly and shuffles to the door. A strange voice calls him by name and bids him enter. He obeys.

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING-ROOM (TRANSFORMED) - CONTINUOUS

His own room, but walls and ceiling hung with living green; bright gleaming berries; holly, mistletoe, ivy; a mighty blaze roaring up the chimney. Heaped on the floor to form a throne: turkeys, geese, game, poultry, brawn, great joints, sucking-pigs, long wreaths of sausages, mince-pies, plum-puddings, barrels of oysters, red-hot chestnuts, cherry-cheeked apples, juicy oranges, luscious pears, immense twelfth-cakes, seething bowls of punch. Upon this couch sits GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT—jolly Giant, glorious, simple green robe bordered with white fur, capacious bare breast, bare feet, holly wreath with shining icicles, long free dark brown curls, genial face, sparkling eye, open hand, antique rusted empty scabbard, glowing torch like Plenty's horn held high.

(SOUND: fire roar, punch seethe, torch crackle)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in! and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me! You have never seen the like of me before!

SCROOGE
Never.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Have never walked forth with the younger members of my family, meaning (for I am very young) my elder brothers born in these later years?

SCROOGE
I don't think I have. I am afraid I have not. Have you had many brothers, Spirit?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
More than eighteen hundred.

SCROOGE
A tremendous family to provide for!

The Ghost rises.

SCROOGE
Spirit, conduct me where you will. I went forth last night on compulsion, and I learnt a lesson which is working now. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

Scrooge does so and holds it fast. Holly, mistletoe, berries, ivy, turkeys, geese, game, poultry, brawn, meat, pigs, sausages, oysters, pies, puddings, fruit, and punch all vanish. So does the room, the fire, the hour of night.

EXT. CITY STREETS ON CHRISTMAS MORNING - DAY

People make rough but brisk music scraping snow from pavement and housetops. Boys delight as it plumps into the road. House fronts black, windows blacker, smooth white sheet of snow on roofs. Poulterers' shops half open, fruiterers radiant—pot-bellied baskets of chestnuts, Spanish onions, pears, apples, grapes, filberts, Norfolk Biffins, oranges, lemons. Grocers nearly closed yet glimpses of scales, twine, canisters, tea, coffee, raisins, almonds, cinnamon, candied fruits, figs, French plums. Customers hurried and eager, tumbling against each other in best humour. Steeples call good people to church. From bye-streets innumerable people carry dinners to the bakers' shops.

(SOUND: snow scrapes, church bells, cheerful calls, basket crashes)

The Ghost and Scrooge stand in a baker's doorway. The Spirit takes off covers as bearers pass and sprinkles incense from his torch. Once or twice when angry words rise between dinner-carriers who jostled, he sheds a few drops of water; good humour is restored at once.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
(to Scrooge's question)
There is a peculiar flavour in what I sprinkle from my torch. My own. It would apply to any kindly given dinner. To a poor one most. Because it needs it most.

SCROOGE
I wonder you, of all the beings in the many worlds about us, should desire to cramp these people's opportunities of innocent enjoyment.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I!

SCROOGE
You would deprive them of their means of dining every seventh day, often the only day on which they can be said to dine at all. You seek to close these places on the Seventh Day?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I seek!

SCROOGE
Forgive me if I am wrong. It has been done in your name, or at least in that of your family.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
There are some upon this earth of yours who lay claim to know us, and who do their deeds of passion, pride, ill-will, hatred, envy, bigotry, and selfishness in our name, who are as strange to us as if they had never lived. Remember that, and charge their doings on themselves, not us.

They go on into the suburbs. The Ghost, though gigantic, accommodates himself to any place with ease.

INT. BOB CRATCHIT'S FOUR-ROOMED HOUSE - DAY

On the threshold the Spirit smiles and stops to bless the dwelling with his torch. Inside: MRS. CRATCHIT—poorly dressed in twice-turned gown but brave in cheap ribbons—lays the cloth, assisted by BELINDA. MASTER PETER plunges a fork into the saucepan of potatoes, collars nearly choking him. Two smaller Cratchits come tearing in, screaming they smelt the goose outside the baker's.

(SOUND: cloth snap, potato bubble, children's screams of joy)

MRS. CRATCHIT
What has ever got your precious father then? And your brother, Tiny Tim! And Martha warn't as late last Christmas Day by half-an-hour?

MARTHA appears. The young Cratchits hustle her to hide. In comes BOB CRATCHIT—threadbare clothes darned and brushed, at least three feet of comforter exclusive of fringe—with TINY TIM upon his shoulder. Tim bears a little crutch; his limbs supported by an iron frame.

BOB CRATCHIT
Why, where's our Martha?

MRS. CRATCHIT
Not coming.

BOB CRATCHIT
Not coming upon Christmas Day!

Martha comes out prematurely from behind the closet door and runs into his arms. The two young Cratchits bear Tim off into the wash-house to hear the pudding singing in the copper.

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. Somehow he gets thoughtful, sitting by himself so much, and thinks the strangest things you ever heard. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

Bob's voice trembles. He says Tim is growing strong and hearty. Tim's active little crutch is heard; back he comes to his stool before the fire. Bob compounds hot mixture in a jug with gin and lemons. Peter and the young Cratchits fetch the goose in high procession.

(SOUND: crutch on floor, goose platter set, gravy hiss)

Such a bustle. Mrs. Cratchit makes gravy hissing hot; Peter mashes potatoes; Belinda sweetens apple-sauce; Martha dusts hot plates; Bob takes Tim beside him; the two young Cratchits set chairs and cram spoons in mouths lest they shriek for goose before their turn. Grace. Breathless pause. Mrs. Cratchit plunges the carving-knife; long-expected gush of stuffing; one murmur of delight. Even Tim beats on the table with his knife handle and feebly cries Hurrah!

There never was such a goose. Eked out by apple-sauce and mashed potatoes, it is a sufficient dinner for the whole family. They haven't ate it all at last! Now the pudding—like a speckled cannon-ball, blazing in half of half-a-quartern of ignited brandy, bedight with Christmas holly.

(SOUND: brandy whoosh, delighted cries)

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

ALL
God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Tim sits very close to his father's side. Bob holds his withered little hand as if he loved the child and dreaded he might be taken from him.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no! Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head to hear his own words, overcome with penitence and grief.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Man, if man you be in heart, not adamant, forbear that wicked cant until you have discovered What the surplus is, and Where it is. Will you decide what men shall live, what men shall die? It may be that in the sight of Heaven you are more worthless and less fit to live than millions like this poor man's child.

Bob proposes Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast. Mrs. Cratchit reddens.

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon, and I hope he'd have a good appetite for it.

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, the children! Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
It should be Christmas Day, I am sure, on which one drinks the health of such an odious, stingy, hard, unfeeling man as Mr. Scrooge. You know he is, Robert! Nobody knows it better than you do, poor fellow!

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his. Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy new year! He'll be very merry and very happy, I have no doubt!

The children drink after her—the first of their proceedings which has no heartiness. Tim drinks last, doesn't care twopence for it. Scrooge is the Ogre of the family. After it passes they are ten times merrier. Bob tells of a situation for Peter; Martha of her milliner's work; they have a song from Tim. They are happy, grateful, pleased with one another, contented with the time. When they fade in the bright sprinklings of the Spirit's torch, Scrooge has his eye upon them, especially on Tiny Tim, until the last.

EXT. BLEAK MOOR - DUSK

Monstrous masses of rude stone, water held prisoner by frost, moss and furze and coarse rank grass. Setting sun leaves a streak of fiery red, then thick gloom of darkest night.

(SOUND: howling wind on barren waste)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
A place where Miners live, who labour in the bowels of the earth. But they know me. See!

A light shines from the window of a hut. They advance, pass through wall of mud and stone. Cheerful company round a glowing fire: old old man and woman, children and children's children, another generation, all decked gaily. The old man sings a Christmas song; they join the chorus.

(SOUND: old song rising over wind)

The Spirit does not tarry. Passing on above the moor, they speed—to sea.

EXT. SOLITARY LIGHTHOUSE ON REEF - NIGHT

Built upon a dismal reef of sunken rocks. Great heaps of sea-weed. Storm-birds rise and fall. Two men watch the light; they have made a fire that sheds a ray through the loophole. They join horny hands over the rough table, wish each other Merry Christmas in their can of grog. The elder, face damaged and scarred, strikes up a sturdy song like a Gale itself.

(SOUND: thundering water, gale-song, fire crackle)

EXT. SHIP AT SEA - NIGHT

Far from any shore. Helmsman at the wheel, look-out in the bow, officers of the watch—dark ghostly figures. Every man hums a Christmas tune or has a Christmas thought or speaks below his breath of some bygone Christmas Day. Every man has had a kinder word for another on that day than on any day in the year.

(SOUND: wind moan, creak of timber, low humming)

Suddenly a hearty laugh. Scrooge recognises it as his nephew's.

INT. FRED'S BRIGHT DINING-ROOM - NIGHT

Bright, dry, gleaming room. FRED holds his sides, rolling his head, twisting his face. Scrooge's niece by marriage—pretty, dimpled, surprised-looking capital face, ripe little mouth, sunniest eyes—laughs as heartily. Assembled friends roar.

(SOUND: contagious laughter, fire crackle, glasses)

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

NIECE
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
He's a comical old fellow. That's the truth. And not so pleasant as he might be. However, his offences carry their own punishment, and I have nothing to say against him. His wealth is of no use to him. He don't do any good with it. He don't make himself comfortable with it. He hasn't the satisfaction of thinking—ha, ha, ha!—that he is ever going to benefit US with it.

NIECE
I have no patience with him.

FRED
Oh, I have! I am sorry for him; I couldn't be angry with him if I tried. Who suffers by his ill whims? Himself, always. Here, he takes it into his head to dislike us, and he won't come and dine with us. What's the consequence? He don't lose much of a dinner.

NIECE
Indeed, I think he loses a very good dinner.

FRED
Well! I'm very glad to hear it. I mean to give him the same chance every year, whether he likes it or not, for I pity him. He may rail at Christmas till he dies, but he can't help thinking better of it—I defy him—if he finds me going there, in good temper, year after year, and saying Uncle Scrooge, how are you? If it only puts him in the vein to leave his poor clerk fifty pounds, that's something.

They play at forfeits, blind-man's buff—Topper always knows where the plump sister in the lace tucker is. Music: Scrooge's niece plays a simple little air on the harp that was familiar to the child who fetched Scrooge from school. Scrooge softens more and more. They play Yes and No. Fred thinks of an animal—live, disagreeable, savage, growls and grunts, talks sometimes, lives in London, walks about the streets, not a horse or ass or cow or bull or tiger or dog or pig or cat or bear.

PLUMP SISTER
I have found it out! I know what it is, Fred! It's your Uncle Scro-o-o-o-oge!

(SOUND: fresh roar of laughter)

FRED
He has given us plenty of merriment, I am sure, and it would be ungrateful not to drink his health. Here is a glass of mulled wine ready to our hand. Uncle Scrooge!

ALL
Well! Uncle Scrooge!

FRED
A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is! He wouldn't take it from me, but may he have it, nevertheless. Uncle Scrooge!

Scrooge has become so gay and light of heart he would have pledged them in return if the Ghost had given him time. The whole scene passes off. They are again upon their travels. The Ghost grows older; hair grey.

SCROOGE
Are spirits' lives so short?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
My life upon this globe is very brief. It ends to-night. To-night at midnight. Hark! The time is drawing near.

The chimes ring three quarters past eleven.

(SOUND: chimes three-quarters)

SCROOGE
Forgive me if I am not justified in what I ask, but I see something strange, and not belonging to yourself, protruding from your skirts. Is it a foot or a claw?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
It might be a claw, for the flesh there is upon it. Look here.

From the foldings of its robe it brings two children: wretched, abject, frightful, hideous, miserable. A boy and girl, yellow, meagre, ragged, scowling, wolfish; prostrate in humility. Where graceful youth should have filled their features, a stale and shrivelled hand has pinched and twisted them. Where angels might have sat enthroned, devils lurk and glare.

(SOUND: children's ragged breath, distant city)

SCROOGE
Spirit! are they yours?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. And they cling to me, appealing from their fathers. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased. Deny it! Slander those who tell it ye! Admit it for your factious purposes, and make it worse. And bide the end!

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve. Scrooge looks about him for the Ghost and sees it not. As the last stroke ceases to vibrate, he remembers Marley's prediction and lifts up his eyes.

(SOUND: twelve deep strokes)

EXT. OPEN PLACE / CITY EDGE - NIGHT

A solemn Phantom, draped and hooded, coming like a mist along the ground toward him. GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME—deep black garment concealing head, face, form; only one outstretched hand visible; tall and stately; mysterious presence filling with solemn dread. Silent.

(SOUND: low wind, slow approach of mist)

SCROOGE
I am in the presence of the Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come? You are about to show me shadows of the things that have not happened, but will happen in the time before us. Is that so, Spirit?

The upper portion of the garment contracts as if the Spirit inclines its head. That is the only answer.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future! I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. But as I know your purpose is to do me good, and as I hope to live to be another man from what I was, I am prepared to bear you company, and do it with a thankful heart. Will you not speak to me?

It gives him no reply. The hand points straight before them.

SCROOGE
Lead on! Lead on! The night is waning fast, and it is precious time to me, I know. Lead on, Spirit!

The Phantom moves away. Scrooge follows in the shadow of its dress.

EXT. THE EXCHANGE / CITY - DAY (FUTURE)

Merchants hurry up and down, chink money in pockets, converse in groups, look at watches, trifle with great gold seals. The Spirit stops beside one little knot. Scrooge advances to listen.

(SOUND: money chink, boots on stone, indifferent voices)

FAT BUSINESSMAN
No, I don't know much about it, either way. I only know he's dead.

SECOND BUSINESSMAN
When did he die?

FAT BUSINESSMAN
Last night, I believe.

THIRD BUSINESSMAN
Why, what was the matter with him? I thought he'd never die.

FAT BUSINESSMAN
God knows.

RED-FACED GENTLEMAN
What has he done with his money?

FAT BUSINESSMAN
I haven't heard. Left it to his company, perhaps. He hasn't left it to me. That's all I know.

General laugh.

FAT BUSINESSMAN
It's likely to be a very cheap funeral, for upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it. Suppose we make up a party and volunteer?

RED-FACED GENTLEMAN
I don't mind going if a lunch is provided. But I must be fed, if I make one.

Another laugh. They stroll away. Scrooge knows the men. The Phantom glides on. Two other wealthy men of business meet.

FIRST MAN
How are you?

SECOND MAN
How are you?

FIRST MAN
Well! Old Scratch has got his own at last, hey?

SECOND MAN
So I am told. Cold, isn't it?

FIRST MAN
Seasonable for Christmas time. You're not a skater, I suppose?

SECOND MAN
No. No. Something else to think of. Good morning!

Not another word. Scrooge looks about for his own image; another man stands in his accustomed corner. He sees no likeness of himself.

EXT. OBSCURE FOUL QUARTER / OLD JOE'S SHOP - NIGHT (FUTURE)

Ways foul and narrow; shops and houses wretched; people half-naked, drunken, slipshod, ugly. Alleys like cesspools. Low-browed beetling shop below a pent-house roof: iron, old rags, bottles, bones, greasy offal. Inside, heaps of rusty keys, nails, chains, hinges, files, scales, weights. OLD JOE—grey-haired rascal nearly seventy—sits by a charcoal stove of old bricks, screened by frousy curtaining of tatters, smoking his pipe.

(SOUND: skreak of rusty hinges, distant drunken shout)

A woman with a heavy bundle slinks in—CHARWOMAN. Another similarly laden—MRS. DILBER the laundress. A man in faded black—UNDERTAKER'S MAN. They burst into a laugh at recognising each other.

CHARWOMAN
Let the charwoman alone to be the first! Let the laundress alone to be the second; and let the undertaker's man alone to be the third. Look here, old Joe, here's a chance! If we haven't all three met here without meaning it!

OLD JOE
You couldn't have met in a better place. Come into the parlour. Stop till I shut the door of the shop. Ah! How it skreeks! There an't such a rusty bit of metal in the place as its own hinges. Ha, ha! We're all suitable to our calling, we're well matched. Come into the parlour.

He rakes the fire with an old stair-rod, trims his smoky lamp with the stem of his pipe. The charwoman throws her bundle on the floor, sits flaunting, elbows on knees.

CHARWOMAN
What odds then! What odds, Mrs. Dilber? Every person has a right to take care of themselves. He always did.

MRS. DILBER
That's true, indeed! No man more so.

CHARWOMAN
Why then, don't stand staring as if you was afraid, woman; who's the wiser? We're not going to pick holes in each other's coats, I suppose?

MRS. DILBER
No, indeed!

UNDERTAKER'S MAN
We should hope not.

CHARWOMAN
Very well, then! Who's the worse for the loss of a few things like these? Not a dead man, I suppose.

MRS. DILBER
No, indeed.

CHARWOMAN
If he wanted to keep 'em after he was dead, a wicked old screw, why wasn't he natural in his lifetime? If he had been, he'd have had somebody to look after him when he was struck with Death, instead of lying gasping out his last there, alone by himself.

MRS. DILBER
It's the truest word that ever was spoke. It's a judgment on him.

CHARWOMAN
I wish it was a little heavier judgment. Open that bundle, old Joe, and let me know the value of it. Speak out plain. I'm not afraid to be the first. Open the bundle, Joe.

The undertaker's man produces his plunder first: seal or two, pencil-case, pair of sleeve-buttons, brooch of no great value. Joe chalks sums on the wall. Mrs. Dilber next: sheets and towels, little wearing apparel, two old-fashioned silver teaspoons, sugar-tongs, few boots. Joe chalks again.

OLD JOE
I always give too much to ladies. It's a weakness of mine, and that's the way I ruin myself. That's your account. If you asked me for another penny, and made it an open question, I'd repent of being so liberal and knock off half-a-crown.

CHARWOMAN
And now undo my bundle, Joe.

Joe goes on his knees, unfastens knots, drags out a large heavy roll of dark stuff.

OLD JOE
What do you call this? Bed-curtains!

CHARWOMAN
Ah! Bed-curtains!

OLD JOE
You don't mean to say you took 'em down, rings and all, with him lying there?

CHARWOMAN
Yes I do. Why not?

OLD JOE
You were born to make your fortune, and you'll certainly do it.

CHARWOMAN
I certainly shan't hold my hand, when I can get anything in it by reaching it out, for the sake of such a man as He was, I promise you, Joe. Don't drop that oil upon the blankets, now.

OLD JOE
His blankets?

CHARWOMAN
Whose else's do you think? He isn't likely to take cold without 'em, I dare say. Ah! You may look through that shirt till your eyes ache; but you won't find a hole in it, nor a threadbare place. It's the best he had, and a fine one too. They'd have wasted it, if it hadn't been for me.

OLD JOE
What do you call wasting of it?

CHARWOMAN
Putting it on him to be buried in, to be sure. Somebody was fool enough to do it, but I took it off again. If calico an't good enough for such a purpose, it isn't good enough for anything. It's quite as becoming to the body. He can't look uglier than he did in that one.

Scrooge listens in horror. They sit grouped about their spoil in the scanty light. He views them with detestation and disgust.

CHARWOMAN
Ha, ha! This is the end of it, you see! He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead! Ha, ha, ha!

(SOUND: her harsh laugh, coins counted on ground)

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way, now. Merciful Heaven, what is this!

INT. DARK BARE ROOM WITH UNCURTAINED BED - NIGHT (FUTURE)

The scene has changed. Scrooge almost touches a bare, uncurtained bed. Beneath a ragged sheet lies something covered up. The room is very dark. A pale light rising in the outer air falls straight upon the bed: plundered and bereft, unwatched, unwept, uncared for—the body of this man.

(SOUND: cat tearing at door, gnawing rats beneath hearth-stone)

The Phantom's steady hand points to the head. The cover is so carelessly adjusted that the slightest raising of it would disclose the face. Scrooge longs to do it but has no power.

SCROOGE
Spirit! this is a fearful place. In leaving it, I shall not leave its lesson, trust me. Let us go!

Still the Ghost points with unmoved finger to the head.

SCROOGE
I understand you, and I would do it if I could. But I have not the power, Spirit. I have not the power. If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

The Phantom spreads its dark robe before him like a wing; withdrawing it, reveals a room by daylight.

INT. DEBTORS' MODEST HOME (CAROLINE) - DAY (FUTURE)

A mother and her children. She walks up and down, starts at every sound, looks out the window, glances at the clock, tries in vain to work with her needle, can hardly bear the children's voices. The long-expected knock. She hurries to the door and meets her husband—careworn and depressed, yet with a remarkable expression of serious delight he struggles to repress.

(SOUND: knock, children's hush, fire tick)

HUSBAND
Is it good? Or bad?

CAROLINE
Bad.

HUSBAND
We are quite ruined?

CAROLINE
No. There is hope yet, Caroline.

HUSBAND
If he relents, there is! Nothing is past hope, if such a miracle has happened.

CAROLINE
He is past relenting. He is dead.

She is thankful in her soul, says so with clasped hands, then prays forgiveness the next moment—but the first was the emotion of her heart.

HUSBAND
What the half-drunken woman whom I told you of last night said to me, when I tried to see him and obtain a week's delay, turns out to have been quite true. He was not only very ill, but dying, then. To whom will our debt be transferred?

CAROLINE
I don't know. But before that time we shall be ready with the money; and even though we were not, it would be a bad fortune indeed to find so merciless a creditor in his successor. We may sleep to-night with light hearts, Caroline!

Yes. Soften it as they would, their hearts are lighter. The children's faces brighter. It is a happier house for this man's death. The only emotion the Ghost can show him, caused by the event, is one of pleasure.

SCROOGE
Let me see some tenderness connected with a death, or that dark chamber, Spirit, which we left just now, will be for ever present to me.

INT. BOB CRATCHIT'S FOUR-ROOMED HOUSE - DAY (FUTURE)

Quiet. Very quiet. The noisy little Cratchits are as still as statues in one corner, looking up at Peter, who has a book before him. The mother and daughters sew. Peter reads:

PETER
"And He took a child, and set him in the midst of them."

Where had Scrooge heard those words? The mother lays her work upon the table and puts her hand up to her face.

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes.

The colour? Ah, poor Tiny Tim!

MRS. CRATCHIT
They're better now again. It makes them weak by candle-light; and I wouldn't show weak eyes to your father when he comes home, for the world. It must be near his time.

PETER
Past it rather. But I think he has walked a little slower than he used, these few last evenings, mother.

They are very quiet again.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I have known him walk with—I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed.

PETER
And so have I. Often.

OTHERS
And so have I.

MRS. CRATCHIT
But he was very light to carry, and his father loved him so, that it was no trouble: no trouble. And there is your father at the door!

She hurries out to meet him. Little Bob in his comforter—he has need of it, poor fellow—comes in. His tea is ready. They all try who shall help him to it most. The two young Cratchits get upon his knees and lay each a little cheek against his face as if they said, "Don't mind it, father. Don't be grieved!"

Bob is very cheerful with them, speaks pleasantly, looks at the work, praises the industry. They would be done long before Sunday, he says.

MRS. CRATCHIT
Sunday! You went to-day, then, Robert?

BOB CRATCHIT
Yes, my dear. I wish you could have gone. It would have done you good to see how green a place it is. But you'll see it often. I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday. My little, little child! My little child!

He breaks down all at once. He leaves the room and goes upstairs into the room above, lighted cheerfully and hung with Christmas. A chair set close beside the child; signs of someone having been there lately. Poor Bob sits down in it, kisses the little face, is reconciled, goes down again quite happy.

(SOUND: quiet sobs, then gentle talk)

They draw about the fire and talk; girls and mother working still. Bob tells of the extraordinary kindness of Mr. Scrooge's nephew, who meeting him in the street and seeing he looked a little down, inquired what had happened, was heartily sorry, gave his card, offered service. It really seemed as if he had known their Tiny Tim and felt with them.

BOB CRATCHIT
I am sure we shall none of us forget poor Tiny Tim—shall we—or this first parting that there was among us?

ALL
Never, father!

BOB CRATCHIT
And I know, I know, my dears, that when we recollect how patient and how mild he was, although he was a little, little child, we shall not quarrel easily among ourselves, and forget poor Tiny Tim in doing it.

ALL
No, never, father!

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very happy. I am very happy!

Mrs. Cratchit kisses him; daughters kiss him; young Cratchits kiss him; Peter and he shake hands.

SCROOGE
Spectre, something informs me that our parting moment is at hand. I know it, but I know not how. Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead?

The Ghost conveys him into the resorts of business men but shows him not himself. Scrooge hastens to the window of his office: it is an office still, but not his. The furniture is not the same; the figure in the chair is not himself. The Phantom points as before. They reach an iron gate.

EXT. CHURCHYARD WITH NEGLECTED GRAVE - DAY (FUTURE)

A churchyard. Walled in by houses; overrun by grass and weeds, the growth of vegetation's death, not life; choked up with too much burying; fat with repleted appetite. A worthy place. The Spirit stands among the graves and points down to One.

(SOUND: wind in weeds, distant city hush)

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

Still the Ghost points downward to the grave by which it stands.

SCROOGE
Men's courses will foreshadow certain ends, to which, if persevered in, they must lead. But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change. Say it is thus with what you show me!

The Spirit is immovable as ever. Scrooge creeps towards it, trembling, and following the finger, reads upon the stone of the neglected grave his own name: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed? No, Spirit! Oh no, no!

The finger points from the grave to him, and back again.

SCROOGE
Spirit! hear me! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. Why show me this, if I am past all hope?

For the first time the hand appears to shake.

SCROOGE
Good Spirit, your nature intercedes for me, and pities me. Assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life!

The kind hand trembles.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. I will not shut out the lessons that they teach. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

In his agony he catches the spectral hand. It seeks to free itself; he is strong in his entreaty and detains it. The Spirit, stronger yet, repulses him. Holding up his hands in a last prayer to have his fate reversed, he sees an alteration in the Phantom's hood and dress. It shrinks, collapses, and dwindles down into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Yes! and the bedpost is his own. The bed is his own, the room is his own. Best and happiest of all, the Time before him is his own, to make amends in!

(SOUND: church bells pealing lustiest clash clang hammer ding dong)

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this! I say it on my knees, old Jacob; on my knees!

He is so fluttered and glowing with good intentions that his broken voice scarcely answers. He has been sobbing violently; his face is wet with tears. He folds one of his bed-curtains in his arms.

SCROOGE
They are not torn down, rings and all. They are here—I am here—the shadows of the things that would have been may be dispelled. They will be. I know they will!

His hands are busy with his garments—turning them inside out, putting them on upside down, tearing them, mislaying them. He laughs and cries in the same breath, a perfect Laocoön with his stockings.

SCROOGE
I don't know what to do! I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy. I am as giddy as a drunken man. A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world. Hallo here! Whoop! Hallo!

He frisks into the sitting-room, perfectly winded.

SCROOGE
There's the saucepan that the gruel was in! There's the door by which the Ghost of Jacob Marley entered! There's the corner where the Ghost of Christmas Present sat! There's the window where I saw the wandering Spirits! It's all right, it's all true, it all happened. Ha ha ha!

Really, for a man who has been out of practice for so many years, it is a splendid laugh, a most illustrious laugh. Running to the window, he opens it and puts out his head. No fog, no mist; clear, bright, jovial, stirring cold; cold piping for the blood to dance to; golden sunlight; heavenly sky; sweet fresh air; merry bells. Oh, glorious! Glorious!

(SOUND: glorious pealing bells, clear cold air)

A boy in Sunday clothes loiters below.

SCROOGE
What's to-day?

BOY
Eh?

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

BOY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it. The Spirits have done it all in one night. They can do anything they like. Of course they can. Hallo, my fine fellow!

BOY
Hallo!

SCROOGE
Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one, at the corner?

BOY
I should hope I did.

SCROOGE
An intelligent boy! A remarkable boy! Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey that was hanging up there?—Not the little prize Turkey: the big one?

BOY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
What a delightful boy! It's a pleasure to talk to him. Yes, my buck!

BOY
It's hanging there now.

SCROOGE
Is it? Go and buy it, and tell 'em to bring it here, that I may give them the direction where to take it. Come back with the man, and I'll give you a shilling. Come back with him in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The boy is off like a shot.

SCROOGE
(whispering, rubbing hands)
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim. Joe Miller never made such a joke as sending it to Bob's will be!

He writes the address with an unsteady hand, goes downstairs to open the street door ready for the poulterer's man. The knocker catches his eye.

SCROOGE
I shall love it as long as I live! I scarcely ever looked at it before. What an honest expression it has in its face! It's a wonderful knocker!—Here's the Turkey! Hallo! Whoop! How are you! Merry Christmas!

It is a Turkey. He never could have stood upon his legs, that bird. He would have snapped them short off in a minute, like sticks of sealing-wax.

SCROOGE
Why, it's impossible to carry that to Camden Town. You must have a cab.

The chuckle with which he says this, and pays for the Turkey, and pays for the cab, and recompenses the boy, is only exceeded by the chuckle with which he sits down breathless in his chair again and chuckles till he cries.

He dresses himself all in his best—clean dark suit of better cut, neat cravat, polished boots—face open and smiling rather than pinched. Shaving is not easy; his hand shakes very much. He gets out into the streets. People pour forth. He regards every one with a delighted smile. Three or four good-humoured fellows say "Good morning, sir! A merry Christmas to you!" Of all the blithe sounds he has ever heard, those are the blithest in his ears.

(SOUND: street bustle, cheerful greetings, his own delighted laugh)

EXT. LONDON STREET - DAY

Coming toward him he beholds the portly gentleman who walked into his counting-house the day before. It sends a pang across his heart, but he knows what path lies straight before him and takes it.

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. It was very kind of you. A merry Christmas to you, sir!

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE
Yes. That is my name, and I fear it may not be pleasant to you. Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness—

He whispers in the gentleman's ear.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you. Will you do me that favour?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
My dear sir, I don't know what to say to such munifi—

SCROOGE
Don't say anything, please. Come and see me. Will you come and see me?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
I will!

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. I am much obliged to you. I thank you fifty times. Bless you!

He goes to church, walks about the streets, watches the people hurrying to and fro, pats children on the head, questions beggars, looks down into kitchens and up to windows, and finds that everything can yield him pleasure. He has never dreamed that any walk—that anything—could give him so much happiness. In the afternoon he turns his steps toward his nephew's house.

EXT. FRED'S HOUSE - AFTERNOON

He passes the door a dozen times before he has the courage to go up and knock. He makes a dash and does it.

(SOUND: hesitant steps, firm knock)

GIRL
(opening)
Yes, sir?

SCROOGE
Is your master at home, my dear?

GIRL
Yes, sir.

SCROOGE
Where is he, my love?

GIRL
He's in the dining-room, sir, along with mistress. I'll show you up-stairs, if you please.

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. He knows me. I'll go in here, my dear.

He turns the lock gently and sidles his face in round the door. They are looking at the table, spread out in great array.

SCROOGE
Fred!

Dear heart alive, how his niece by marriage starts!

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Let him in! It is a mercy he doesn't shake his arm off. He is at home in five minutes. Nothing could be heartier. His niece looks just the same. So does Topper when he comes. So does the plump sister. So does every one. Wonderful party, wonderful games, wonderful unanimity, wonderful happiness!

(SOUND: joyful welcome, laughter, chair scrapes, music later)

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING-HOUSE - NEXT MORNING

Scrooge is early there—oh, he is early there. If he could only be there first and catch Bob Cratchit coming late! That is the thing he has set his heart upon. And he does it. The clock strikes nine. No Bob. A quarter past. No Bob. He is full eighteen minutes and a half behind his time. Scrooge sits with his door wide open that he may see him come into the Tank.

(SOUND: clock strike nine, then quarter, expectant silence)

Bob's hat is off before he opens the door; his comforter too. He is on his stool in a jiffy, driving away with his pen as if trying to overtake nine o'clock.

SCROOGE
(in accustomed growl)
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? Yes. I think you are. Step this way, sir, if you please.

BOB CRATCHIT
It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated. I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend. I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore—

He leaps from his stool and digs Bob in the waistcoat so that Bob staggers back into the Tank.

SCROOGE
and therefore I am about to raise your salary!

Bob trembles, and gets a little nearer to the ruler. He has a momentary idea of knocking Scrooge down with it, holding him, and calling for help and a strait-waistcoat.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon, over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

(SOUND: Scrooge's hearty clap on Bob's back, Bob's astonished breath)

Scrooge is better than his word. He does it all, and infinitely more; and to Tiny Tim, who did NOT die, he is a second father. He becomes as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man as the good old city knows, or any other good old city, town, or borough in the good old world. Some people laugh to see the alteration in him, but he lets them laugh, and little heeds them. His own heart laughs: and that is quite enough for him.

EXT. LONDON / VARIOUS - MONTAGE - DAY TO YEAR

Scrooge keeps Christmas well. No further intercourse with Spirits; he lives upon the Total Abstinence Principle ever afterwards. Tiny Tim runs with his crutch, strong. Fred welcomes him. Bob thrives. The streets at Christmas fill with light.

(SOUND: bells, laughter, Tim's crutch now lively, fading to quiet joy)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was always said of him, that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge. May that be truly said of us, and all of us! And so, as Tiny Tim observed, God bless Us, Every One!

FADE OUT.

THE END

